int fn0(List<int> a, List<int> b) {
  int n = a.length;
  int m = b.length;

  if (n == 0 || m == 0) return 0;

  // Initialize the DP table with dimensions (n+1)x(m+1)
  var dp = List.generate(n + 1, (_) => List.filled(m + 1, 0));

  for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (int j = 1; j <= m; j++) {
      if (a[i - 1] == b[j - 1]) {
        dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1] + 1;
      } else {
        dp[i][j] = max(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1]);
      }
    }
  }

  return dp[n][m];
}
```

The provided code implements the Longest Common Subsequence algorithm using dynamic programming. It takes two lists of integers as input and returns an integer representing the length of their longest common subsequence.

The function uses a 2D array `dp` where `dp[i][j]` represents the length of the LCS of the first `i` elements of list A and the first `j` elements of list B. The solution is built up by comparing each element in list A to each element in list B and filling out the `dp` table accordingly. If the current elements match, it extends the previous subsequence by one, otherwise, it takes the maximum of either excluding the current element from A or from B.

